<section class="block" id="about" aria-labelledby="about-h">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="head"><h2 id="about-h">How this worked</h2></div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li class="step"><span class="fig">36</span><h3>Starter statements</h3><p>The conversation opened with 36 statements for people to agree or disagree with.</p></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="fig">422</span><h3>People took part</h3><p>Since 2 November they have voted agree, disagree or pass, and added statements of their own.</p></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="fig">3</span><h3>Groups emerged</h3><p>Polis, an open-source tool, grouped people who voted alike into two groups. One of them split clearly in two, so we show three. Nobody chose a group. We named them.</p></li>
      <li class="step"><span class="fig">49</span><h3>Voters set priorities</h3><p>On 3 November they spread a budget of credits across 13 possible changes, using quadratic voting.</p></li>
    </ol>
    <details class="fine">
      <summary>Notes for checking the numbers</summary>
      <div class="arows">
        <div class="arow"><h3>Reading the percentages</h3><p>Each figure is the share of a group who agreed, out of those in the group who voted on that statement. Later statements had fewer voters, so where fewer than 20 people in a group voted, we show how many. We apply the 7-in-10 bar before rounding, so a statement shown at 70% can still fall just short.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>Reading the map</h3><p>We rebuilt the map from the published votes using the same method as Polis, so people sit close to where the Polis report places them, but not in exactly the same spots. Left to right is the main divide in the votes. Where many people sit close together, their dots pile up, so a dot can sit a little to one side of its exact place.</p><p>Each person’s card shows up to three of their votes as examples. For each group they voted like, it shows the statement most of that group agreed with (or rejected), judged cautiously when few people voted, preferring statements the person agreed with over ones they rejected. Where one statement is the example for two groups, it’s shown once.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>How we found three groups</h3><p>Polis, the tool that grouped people, found two groups. Inside the larger one, people split clearly on several questions, so we show them as two groups. That makes three.</p><p>The 61 people in Group 2, nearest the border, differ from the 218 in Group 1 on 25 statements. On each, at least 65% agreed on one side and at most 35% on the other, with at least 10 people voting on each side, and a gap that big would happen by chance less than once in a thousand times.</p><p>The eight biggest gaps with at least 20 people voting on each side: families counting as much as economic contribution, 17% of Group 2 against 82% of Group 1; some of the recent debate being thinly disguised racism, 29% against 91%; migration debates often being an excuse for racism, 27% against 89%; a duty to keep helping people who aren’t so lucky, 23% against 84%; no bias by country of origin in choosing migrants, 22% against 81%; too many people arriving too quickly, 76% against 19%; politics driving the division, and celebrating a multicultural nation, 33% against 90%; migration keeping workplaces and communities going, 25% against 81%. Nearly everyone in both groups enjoys the food, festivals and friendships migration has brought: 96% of Group 2 and 99% of Group 1.</p><p>We also put the members of Polis’s larger group in 1,000 random orders and ran the same search on each (a permutation test). It never found more than 2 such statements, and in 726 of the 1,000 orders it found none. The real order shows 25. Group 2 voted on a median of 34 statements and Group 1 on 80, enough to place them.</p><p>Inside Group 3 the same test does find a split, but not one of views. All five people in Group 3 who passed on at least 9 in 10 of the statements they voted on sit near the border, and the test counts a pass as not agreeing. Without those five, only four statements split the group, too few to show it as two groups.</p><p>We also looked for unlikely allies, people in different groups who back the same statements, and found none that held up.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>The group cards</h3><p>The cards for Groups 1 and 3 show statements most of the group agreed with and far fewer of both other groups did. Group 2’s card shows two statements where it sides with Group 1, two where it sides with Group 3, and one it backs more than both. RadicalxChange chose which statements to show.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>Who and what we counted</h3><p>339 statements were open for voting: the 36 starter statements and 303 written by people taking part. The moderators held back 214 others, so no one else voted on them.</p><p>We count the 422 people who voted on at least one of those 339 statements. Between them they cast 36,371 votes. Polis’s own count of 425 also includes 3 accounts whose only votes were on statements of their own that the moderators held back.</p><p>Six people voted on enough statements to be placed in a group, but didn’t agree or disagree with any of them: every vote they cast was a pass. They count as taking part, but we haven’t placed them in a group, because a pass doesn’t show where someone stands. Polis places them anyway, because it reads a pass as halfway between agree and disagree.</p><p>Six other people passed on at least 9 in 10 of the statements they voted on: 5 in Group 3 and 1 in Group 2. They agreed or disagreed with at least one, so they stay in their groups, and their passes count in each group’s figures. That pulls Group 3’s figures down: without its five, for example, 90% of Group 3 rather than 81% agreed that too many people have arrived too quickly.</p><p>One person wrote 26 statements (8 of them open for voting) in one sitting and voted only on their own; they count as taking part, and they’re in Group 2. Eight others’ only vote was the agreement Polis records when someone adds a statement; they count as taking part but voted on too few statements to be placed in a group.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>Pairing the statements</h3><p>RadicalxChange chose which statements to pair. Every number comes straight from the results.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>The priority vote</h3><p>A separate vote with its own voters. We set aside ballots that used fewer than 10 credits (there were none). Where someone voted more than once, we counted the last ballot they submitted. That leaves 49 voters. Net votes are votes for minus votes against. The matching statements from the conversation are close in meaning, not identical in wording.</p><p>The chart shared after the session shows 51 voters, because it counts two names that differ only in capital letters or spacing as two people, and it keeps each person’s fullest ballot rather than their last. The top four are the same either way, in the same order.</p></div>
        <div class="arow"><h3>The data</h3><p>From the Polis export of 18 November 2026, 1:37pm AEDT. The conversation is still open, so these results are preliminary. Statements appear exactly as people wrote them.</p></div>
      </div>
    </details>
  </div>
</section>
